// #3147/#3176: the per-span occupied-slot bitmap — sizing, addressing and a
// test-only reader. Sibling of `./alloc.bit`, same module (SPEC §17.1):
// `allocSpanBytes`, `allocSpanHdrWords`, `allocSpanOf`, `allocWordAt`,
// `allocClassSize`, `allocSpanSlots` and `spanSkip` are all declared there and
// visible here with no import, exactly as `./classify.bit` and
// `./spancache.bit` already rely on. Split out purely to keep `alloc.bit`
// under `_tests_/bit/lintfilelines.bit`'s 800-line gate (E0200) — it was
// already at exactly that limit before #3176 (see `./spancache.bit`'s
// header). This seam declares no const, class or type of its own, so there
// is no ordering question, only the diagnostic-order one the differentials
// cover (`bitlang-ws/CLAUDE.md`, "Splitting a file").
//
// THE FULL DESIGN, INCLUDING WHY WRITES LIVE IN `runtime/gc/` AND NOT HERE, IS
// `../gc/gcindex.bit`'s design comment. In one line: the bitmap is a
// per-class extension of the span's reserved header prefix (`spanSkip`,
// `./alloc.bit`), sized off the slot count BEFORE the bitmap's own bytes
// remove any (avoiding a circular sizing dependency), and it is set/cleared
// only by `runtime/gc/`'s `linkObject`/`freeObject` — `runtime/alloc/` cannot
// tell a real object from `gcInit`'s mark worklist, which allocates through
// this heap without ever becoming a tracked object.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// #3925 FIXED THIS: THE BITMAP IS ZEROED WHEN A SPAN LEAVES THE #3045 CACHE
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `heapRefillLocked` (`./alloc.bit`) initialises the ten header words and the
// free list and stops — it always did, and still does. Nothing there zeroes
// the bitmap, so a span that has been through the #3045 cache and is
// re-carved FOR A DIFFERENT CLASS would start life with stale bits set.
// #3177's reader is what would have read them (`gcOwns`, `../gc/gcalloc.bit`);
// it was reverted before this landed, so nothing was ever provably corrupted
// by it, but the fix could not wait for a second reader to land first.
//
// WHY IT HAPPENS. The bitmap sits at a fixed offset and its extent depends on
// the class: `allocBitmapWords` is LARGEST for the SMALLEST class (class 0: 256
// words, ending at byte 2128) and smallest for the largest (class 36: 1 word,
// ending at byte 88). A class's first slot, `spanSkip(cs) * cs`, starts as low
// as byte 192 (class 10). So for 24 of the 37 classes the old slot area
// overlaps a subsequent class-0 bitmap — and a reclaimed span's slots hold
// free-list `next` POINTERS, never zeroes. Overlaps, in bytes, against class
// 0's 2128-byte bitmap: class 1 1024, class 2 1520, class 3 1696, class 4 1744,
// class 10 1936 (the worst), ... class 24 80. Classes 25-36 do not overlap.
//
// WHAT IT COSTS IF UNFIXED. Those stale bits read as OCCUPIED, so `gcOwns`
// answers true for slots that hold nothing, `gcMarkRoot` decodes a header 32
// bytes below a free-list link, and the collector marks and later sweeps
// garbage as if it were an object. NO CRASH HAS BEEN ATTRIBUTED TO THIS: #3388
// measured 59 of 104 span reuses starting with a non-zero bitmap (608 stale
// words) on one `bench/cases/json` run, then traced that benchmark's SIGSEGV
// to a different cause — #3177's reader poisoning `gcOwns`'s object-membership
// table with span-base keys, not this bitmap — and confirmed the exclusion
// directly: zeroing the bitmap at carve time on the crashing tree still
// crashed 16/16. #3925 re-measured the same benchmark with a counting probe
// in `heapRefillLocked` (carves total, carves whose bitmap arrived dirty,
// checked before anything overwrites it): **3295 carves, 57 dirty**,
// deterministic across repeated runs, and **0 of the same 3295 dirty** after
// this fix — same binary otherwise, byte-identical stdout.
//
// WHY `_tests_/stress/gcslotbitmap` CANNOT CATCH IT. It keeps one object alive
// per span precisely so no span is ever reclaimed, and its arena is
// caller-bound, so `reserveHeadOwns` reports `owned = 0` and
// `heapReclaimSpansLocked` skips every span it owns. No span in that fixture is
// ever re-carved for a different class. Any guard for this needs spans the
// RESERVE mapped.
//
// WHY THE FIX IS IN `./spancache.bit`, NOT HERE. The obvious fix — zero
// `allocBitmapWords(cs)` words in `heapRefillLocked`, unconditionally — was
// measured on `bench/cases/json` against the same tree without it, interleaved
// run-by-run so both saw the same machine. json's retired-instruction count is
// BIMODAL at roughly 99G and 124G (the cheap mode is the run where the index
// grows one step less). The minimum barely moves (+0.4% to +0.9% over 24
// paired runs), but the zeroing roughly HALVES the probability of the cheap
// mode — 17 of 24 paired runs cheap without it, 7 of 24 with it — which is
// about +25% on the median. Paying that on EVERY refill for a structure that
// is already clean on two of its three sources is the wrong trade.
//
// The fix instead scopes to the one source that needs it: `reserveGrow` hands
// back fresh `mmap` memory (already zero), the #3868 free list is remapped by
// `spanFreeListPush` before it is ever handed back (already zero), and only a
// span popped off the #3045 cache (`heapSpanCacheTakeLocked`) can carry a
// previous class's bits — so that is where it is zeroed, in `./spancache.bit`,
// on the way out, before `heapRefillLocked` ever sees it. That also means
// `heapRefillLocked` cannot know the span's origin (it is not told, and
// `../gc/gc.bit`'s call site is not this module to change), so the width
// zeroed is class 0's — the widest of any class, since class 0 has the most
// slots — which is a superset of whatever the destination class turns out to
// be. Re-measured the same way after scoping it: **min wall-clock identical**
// to the control (1.410s of 28 paired runs each), median +2.4%, mean +5.4% —
// both within ordinary run-to-run noise on a box that was not fully idle, and
// nothing like the unscoped form's +25% median. `_tests_/stress/alloc`'s own
// direct callers of `heapRefillLocked` bypass the cache entirely (`spanTake`,
// a bump arena), so this scoping does not touch their carve-path timing at
// all — see `./alloc.bit`'s header comment above `heapRefillLocked` for the
// exact reasoning it did not fit here instead.

// 64-bit words needed for class `cs`'s occupied-slot bitmap (one bit per
// slot). Sized off `naive(cs) = allocSpanBytes / cs`, the slot count BEFORE
// `spanSkip`'s reservation removes any — sizing off the post-reservation
// count would be circular, and `naive(cs)` is a safe upper bound because
// `spanSkip` only ever shrinks the usable count further.
@nosplit fn allocBitmapWords(cs: int): int {
  let naive = allocSpanBytes / cs
  return (naive + 63) / 64
}

// The pure addressing primitive the occupied-slot bitmap is built on —
// mirrors `allocSpanOf` in taking a raw address and nothing else the caller
// must already know except which class it belongs to. Returns the slot index
// of `addr` within its span for class `idx`, or -1 when `addr` is NOT the
// exact base of a slot (out of the span's carved range, or an interior offset
// into one).
//
// THE REMAINDER CHECK IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT DEFENSIVE. Floor division alone
// (`off / cs`) aliases every interior address inside a slot's `cs` bytes onto
// that slot's own index — an interior pointer and its slot's base would
// otherwise test identically. `gcOwns`'s contract (ABI.md §3, and the design
// note in `../gc/gcindex.bit`) requires interior pointers to answer false, so
// the `off % cs != 0` check must run BEFORE the bit is trusted by any future
// caller. This ticket's own callers (`linkObject`/`freeObject`, always exact
// slot bases from the allocator itself) never hit the -1 case; a future
// `gcOwns` caller (#3177) reading an arbitrary candidate pointer will.
// EACH PER-CLASS QUANTITY IS COMPUTED ONCE (#4044). `cs` and `spanSkip(cs)` are
// pure functions of `idx` — one of 37 values — and this used to derive both
// TWICE: once here and once inside `allocSpanSlots`, which re-calls
// `allocClassSize` and `spanSkip` from the same `idx`. Disassembly at
// `94cc0cb3` showed four out-of-line calls and nine `sdiv` on the taken path,
// measured at 162.03 instructions/call. `allocSpanSlots`'s body is one line —
// `allocSpanBytes / cs - spanSkip(cs)` — so it is spelled out here from the
// values already in hand rather than called.
//
// THAT IS A COPY OF A ONE-LINE FORMULA, and copies drift. `_tests_/stress/alloc`
// property (n) (`./slotindex.bit`) pins the two together directly: for every
// class it asks this function about the last carved slot and about the address
// one slot past it, so a divergence between the inlined count and the exported
// `allocSpanSlots` reddens rather than silently mis-bounding the bitmap.
//
// The divides are NOT the cost and must not be the target: 162 instructions
// retiring in 19.11 cycles is 8.48 IPC, so the `sdiv`s are absorbed by ILP.
// The same mis-framing was corrected for `ldar` on #4038.
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_slot_index") fn allocSlotIndex(addr: int, idx: int): int {
  let cs = allocClassSize(idx)
  let skip = spanSkip(cs)
  let carveStart = allocSpanOf(addr) + skip * cs
  let off = addr - carveStart
  if (off < 0) { return -1 }
  if (off % cs != 0) { return -1 }
  let n = off / cs
  if (n >= allocSpanBytes / cs - skip) { return -1 }   // == allocSpanSlots(idx)
  return n
}

// Word `wordIdx` of the span's occupied-slot bitmap, immediately after the
// 10-word header — the same "read by address, so a test can compute occupancy
// independently" pattern `allocSpanClass`/`allocSpanTotal`/`allocSpanFree`
// (`./alloc.bit`) already use. Also the actual production reader now (#3177):
// `gcOwns` calls this for every span-backed query, after confirming the span
// is presence-keyed. `@symbol`-pinned for the same reason `allocSpanClass`
// is — a cross-module call under `--freestanding` (ABI.md §9).
export @nosplit @symbol("bit_rt_alloc_span_bitmap_word") fn allocSpanBitmapWord(spanAddr: int, wordIdx: int): int {
  let w = allocWordAt(spanAddr + allocSpanHdrWords * 8 + wordIdx * 8)
  return *w
}
